Then $x(t) = A \cos (2 t) + B \sin (2 t)$ for some constants $A$ and $B$.
We have
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \frac{d^{4} x}{d t^{4}} &= -16 A \cos (2 t) - 16 B \sin (2 t) \\ \frac{d^{4} x}{d t^{4}} - x &= -16 A \cos (2 t) - 16 B \sin (2 t) - (A \cos (2 t) + B \sin (2 t)) \\ &= (-17 A - B) \cos (2 t) - (16 B + A) \sin (2 t) \end{align*}
We want this to be equal to $\cos (2 t)$, so we must have
\begin{align*} -17 A - B &= 1 \\ 16 B + A &= 0 \end{align*}
From the second equation, $A = -16 B$. Substituting into the first equation, we get
\begin{align*} -17 (-16 B) - B &= 1 \\ 272 B - B &= 1 \\ 271 B &= 1 \\ B &= \frac{1}{271} \end{align*}
Then $A = -16 B = -16 \left( \frac{1}{271} \right) = -\frac{16}{271}$.
Therefore, the solution is
\begin{align*} x(t) &= A \cos (2 t) + B \sin (2 t) \\ &= -\frac{16}{271} \cos (2 t) + \frac{1}{271} \sin (2 t) \end{align*}

